<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Rawa</h1><p class="st-meta">1313 CE – 1462 CE (approx.) · capital Rawa Mazowiecka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Masovian duchy in central Poland, one of the last Piast lands, incorporated into the Polish Crown in 1462.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-ii-of-masovia">Boleslaw II of Masovia</a> died in 1313, his lands were divided among his sons, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siemowit-ii-of-masovia">Siemowit II</a> received the southern part around Rawa. Over the next 150 years Rawa was repeatedly joined to and separated from the other Masovian duchies as the family's branches divided their inheritance.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Rawa was a modest farming land with a brick castle built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III</a>'s influence in the 14th century. Its dukes were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Polish Crown from 1351. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siemowit-iii-of-masovia">Siemowit III</a> reunited most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> in the 1370s and issued laws for it, but it was divided again among his heirs, and Rawa was ruled by the Siemowit and Wladyslaw lines of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-plock">Plock</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Siemowit VI and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-of-plock">Wladyslaw II</a>, the last dukes of the line, died in 1462, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">King Casimir IV</a> took Rawa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gostynin">Gostynin</a> into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1385-1569">Kingdom of Poland</a> (1385-1569). The land became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rawa-voivodeship">Rawa Voivodeship</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-rawa.jpg" alt="Duchy of Rawa" width="320" height="198"><figcaption>Image: Poznaniak · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chor%C4%85giew_ksi%C4%99cia_mazowieckiego_Siemowita.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1313 CE – 1462 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rawa Mazowiecka</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Siemowit II, Siemowit III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rawa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1388&amp;focus=duchy-of-rawa" aria-label="Duchy of Rawa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5137 387 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Rawa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5457 643L5442 621M5457 643L5457 692"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5442 621h0M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5457 643h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5457 643h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rawa Mazowiecka. <a href="/atlas?year=1388&amp;focus=duchy-of-rawa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
